{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims §11.3 flags are sustained because the adjudication-ritual 'penalizes opacity rather than absence of causal reasoning.' But the problem context defines the standard as auditing whether 'adjudication-ritual flags sustained (§11.3)' are justified — the target never identifies what specific §11.3 flag was raised, what decision boundary was allegedly conflated, or how the ritual's actual adjudication text conflates these two distinct failures. Without citing the ritual's own reasoning or the specific flag text, the claim that flags are 'sustained' is an unsupported assertion: it describes a conceptual concern about opacity-vs-absence without connecting it to any concrete defect in the adjudication-ritual as enacted. The target provides no commitments and no evidence, making its verdict that 'flags are sustained' indistinguishable from a restatement of the problem's premise rather than an independent audit of it.","target":"675c673e8647d2631f92668e21a3142b6d7fbce81a7ecb089ceb83f7fcac9bd0"}]}